# Where every address on the old site goes on this one.
#
# knowledgegraph.tech ran on WordPress until this site took the domain over.
# That site published 926 addresses that search engines, old newsletters,
# conference programmes and other people's links still point at. Without this
# file every one of them would answer "page not found" the moment the domain
# moves, and the search ranking built up since 2019 would go with them.
#
# Each line says: old address, new address, 301. A 301 is the permanent kind of
# redirect, which is the one that passes a page's search standing on to its
# replacement. Nobody sees these lines. A visitor just lands on the right page.
#
# Netlify reads the file top to bottom and stops at the first line that matches,
# so the exact addresses come first and the catch-alls for whole sections come
# last. A trailing slash is ignored when matching, so /about-kgc and
# /about-kgc/ both take the same line.
#
# Ten addresses are deliberately absent because this site already answers them
# unchanged: /, /agenda, /blog, /call-for-posters, /code-of-conduct, /community,
# /hcls, /kgc-lifetime-achievement-awards, /startup-pitch, /team and /tickets.
# So are the 70 blog articles: their addresses were copied from the old site on
# purpose, so /blog/<article> already lands on the matching page here.
#
# Adding a line: put it above the catch-alls, keep the three columns, use 301.
# Full reasoning, and what still has no good home, is in
# docs/audit-2026-09-19/domain/MOVE-TO-DOMAIN.md.

